This plugin follows the shape of other open source work. No code is copied from it; where a
structure, a format or a style is followed, it is named here.

1. anthropics/knowledge-work-plugins, the "data" plugin
   https://github.com/anthropics/knowledge-work-plugins
   Apache License 2.0, published by Anthropic.
   Followed: the marketplace and plugin layout (.claude-plugin/marketplace.json, plugins/<name>/,
   .claude-plugin/plugin.json, .mcp.json with a remote "bigquery" server at
   https://bigquery.googleapis.com/mcp, skills/<name>/SKILL.md). Changed: that plugin lists only the
   BigQuery URL, which cannot sign in from Claude Code because Google's remote MCP servers support
   neither Dynamic Client Registration nor Client ID Metadata Documents; this plugin adds a
   headersHelper (scripts/gcloud-token) and documents an OAuth client of your own.

2. anthropics/skills, the Apache 2.0 skills (skill-creator and mcp-builder)
   https://github.com/anthropics/skills
   Apache License 2.0, published by Anthropic.
   Followed: the SKILL.md style, with name and description front matter, a description that says
   what the skill does and when to use it, and short instructions in the imperative. Nothing is taken
   from the docx, pdf, pptx or xlsx skills, which are not open source.

3. PostHog wizard, the web-analytics-doctor program
   https://github.com/PostHog/wizard (src/programs/web-analytics-doctor/index.ts)
   MIT License, Copyright (c) 2025 PostHog.
   Followed in commands/doctor.md: the shape of the check, which audits read only first, then lists
   the findings and lets the person pick which fixes to make, and applies only those. No text is
   copied. The skill text behind that program (PostHog/context-mill) carries no license and was not
   used.

4. Model Context Protocol Registry, server.json (repository root, not part of this plugin folder)
   https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/registry
   Schema: https://static.modelcontextprotocol.io/schemas/2025-12-11/server.schema.json
   Apache License 2.0 (code being relicensed from MIT) and CC-BY-4.0 (documentation).
   Followed: the server.json format for a remote server with a secret header.
